===BODY===
          <h2>Clicking, or nothing at all</h2>
          <p>A single click, rapid clicking, or dim dashboard lights when you try to start usually point to a <strong>flat or failing battery</strong>, or a poor connection at the battery terminals. It's the most common cause, especially after a cold night, a long period without driving, or lights left on. A jump start often fixes it. See our guide: <a href="{{R}}guides/flat-battery-jump-start-or-recovery.html">jump start or recovery?</a></p>

          <h2>No lights and no sound</h2>
          <p>If nothing lights up at all, the battery may be completely flat or disconnected. On cars with keyless entry, also check the battery in the key fob: many cars let you start with a dead fob by holding it against a marked spot on the steering column or the start button (your handbook shows where).</p>

          <h2>Engine turns over but won't fire</h2>
          <p>If the engine cranks normally but doesn't start, the battery is probably fine. Common causes are a fuel problem (an empty tank, a failed fuel pump or the <a href="{{R}}guides/wrong-fuel.html">wrong fuel</a>), an ignition fault or a sensor failure. Don't keep cranking for long periods: it drains the battery and won't fix the fault.</p>

          <h2>A key or lock symbol on the dashboard</h2>
          <p>A flashing key or padlock symbol usually means the <strong>immobiliser</strong> doesn't recognise the key. Try the spare key if you have it. If the spare works, the problem is the first key.</p>

          <h2>Quick checks before you call</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Automatic: is the gear selector fully in <strong>P</strong>? Try N as well.</li>
            <li>Manual: is the clutch pressed all the way down? Many cars need it.</li>
            <li>Steering lock: turn the wheel gently while turning the key or pressing start.</li>
            <li>Fuel: is the gauge really above empty? Some gauges read wrongly when very low.</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Stop if you hear grinding, see smoke or smell fuel.</strong> Don't keep trying. Get everyone away from the car and call for help. Call 999 if there's any sign of fire.</p>
          </div>

          <h2>When to call for recovery</h2>
          <p>If a jump start doesn't work, the car cranks but won't fire, or there's a warning light you don't recognise, it needs a garage. Call or WhatsApp us with the symptoms: if roadside help is likely to work we'll say so, and if not we'll recover the car to your garage or home. Local recovery starts from £125.</p>
===FAQ===
Q: Why does my car click but not start?
A: Clicking almost always means the starter motor isn't getting enough power, usually because of a flat or weak battery or a loose battery terminal. A jump start often gets you going.
Q: My car turns over but won't start. Is it the battery?
A: Probably not. If the engine cranks at normal speed, the battery is usually fine. The cause is more likely fuel, ignition or a sensor. It needs diagnosing at a garage.
Q: Can you jump start my car instead of towing it?
A: Often, yes, if the problem is a flat battery. Tell us the symptoms when you call and we'll tell you honestly whether a jump start is likely to work.
